2026-09-17 01:14:22 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\n        You are a workflow assistant. Extract parameters for the intent "select_idea_by_reference".\n\n        User message:\n        select this: Students for Fair Admissions v. University of NC\n\n        Parameter schema for this intent:\n        - "reference": type=string, required=True\n\n        Extraction instructions for this intent:\n        - The "reference" parameter must always be set to the idea\'s "id" field.\n- Never return the idea name or description.\n- Use the relevant context block (which lists ideas with id, name, and description)\n  to determine which idea the user is referring to.\n- If the user refers to an idea by name, description, or position (e.g., "the immigration case",\n  "idea 3", "the fed reserve governor case"), resolve it to the correct idea and return its id.\n\n\n\nRelevant context:\nideas:\n  - id: 21-887, name: Perez v. Sturgis Public Schools, description: The case is about whether a settlement agreement under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) extinguishes separate rights to money damages under the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), after a student was denied an education and suffered permanent harm.\n  - id: 24-38, name: Little v. Hecox, description: The case Little v. Hecox concerns whether Idaho\'s law, which classifies athletes by sex rather than gender identity, unfairly discriminates against transgender males who identify as female and wish to compete in women\'s sports.\n  - id: 20-322, name: Garland, Att\'y Gen. v. Gonzalez, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a lower court can issue a class-wide injunction to stop certain immigration provisions, and if so, under what circumstances.\n  - id: 21-147, name: Egbert v. Boule, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether to expand a legal doctrine known as Bivens, which allows individuals to sue federal officials for damages in certain cases. The Court is weighing whether to apply Bivens to new types of claims, including First Amendment retaliation and Fourth Amendment border security cases.\n  - id: 20-1199, name: Students for Fair Admissions v. President and Fellows of Harvard, description: The case centers on the use of racial preferences in university admissions, with one side arguing that such practices are necessary for diversity and the other claiming they unfairly disadvantage certain groups, particularly Asians.\n  - id: 21-476, name: 303 Creative LLC v. Elenis, description: The central dispute in this case is whether a public accommodations law that requires businesses to provide equal access to their services violates the First Amendment. The company claims that requiring them to serve gay couples infringes on their expression, but the law actually targets discriminatory sales and its effect on expression is incidental. If granted a pre-enforcement order, the company could turn away all gay couples and potentially empower other businesses to refuse service based on various characteristics.\n  - id: 20-255, name: Mahanoy Area School Dist. v. B. L., description: The case centers on whether the landmark Supreme Court decision Tinker applies to speech that occurs off school grounds, particularly on social media, and whether schools can restrict such speech without infringing on students\' First Amendment rights.\n  - id: 20-804, name: Houston Community College Sys. v. Wilson, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a member of an elected body can sue for retaliation after being censured by their peers, with the issue centered on the First Amendment\'s protection of free speech versus the right to criticize and respond to a member\'s actions.\n  - id: 20-1088, name: Carson v. Makin, description: The case of Carson v. Makin concerns Maine\'s sectarian exclusion policy, which discriminates against students and schools based on their religious affiliation. Petitioners argue that this policy is unconstitutional, as it fails to meet the standard of strict scrutiny. They contend that religious schools are excluded solely because of their religious nature, making the policy a form of discrimination based on religion.\n  - id: 23-997, name: Stanley v. City of Sanford, description: The case of Stanley v. City of Sanford concerns whether former employees can challenge discrimination in post-employment benefits under the Americans with Disabilities Act. The issue revolves around whether employees who were discriminated against while still employed can sue for such discrimination, even after they have left their job.\n  - id: 18-1171, name: Comcast Corp. v. National Ass. of African American-Owned Media, description: Comcast Corp. is challenging a Ninth Circuit decision that allows plaintiffs to succeed in employment discrimination cases under Section 1981 by showing race was a factor, even if not the sole reason for a business decision.\n  - id: 23-1039, name: Ames v. OH Dept. of Youth Services, description: The case, Ames v. OH Dept. of Youth Services, concerns whether a judge-made rule known as the \'background circumstances rule\' is consistent with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, which aims to eradicate workplace discrimination. The rule in question requires courts to apply a categorical evidentiary presumption against majority-group plaintiffs, potentially allowing discrimination based on protected characteristics. The Court is asked to reverse a lower court\'s judgment that upheld this rule.\n  - id: 20-219, name: Cummings v. Premier Rehab, description: The case is about whether victims of intentional discrimination can receive emotional distress damages under certain federal laws, with the Supreme Court considering a lower court\'s decision that such damages are not available.\n  - id: 19-1257, name: Brnovich v. Democratic National Committee, description: The case is about whether Arizona\'s voting laws deny minority voters an equal opportunity to cast their votes, despite being eligible and registered to vote.\n  - id: 21-707, name: Students for Fair Admissions v. University of NC, description: The case centers on the constitutionality of considering race in college admissions, with one side arguing it\'s necessary for diversity and another claiming it violates equal protection under the law.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
